 ## Overview
-This family encompasses the differentiable rendering mechanisms and architectural extensions that convert 3D Gaussian primitives into 2D images, enabling both standard novel view synthesis and advanced inverse rendering tasks [G]. It solves the problem of efficiently rasterizing soft, view-dependent volumetric primitives while supporting complex appearance models such as physical lighting and volume data visualization [G]. What sets these methods apart from standard [3D Gaussian Splatting](1795-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) is the decoupling of geometric representation from appearance, allowing for explicit material properties (BRDF) or scalar density fields (VEG) rather than baked spherical harmonics [S1, S2, S1, unofficial].
+This family encompasses the differentiable rendering mechanisms and architectural extensions that convert 3D Gaussian primitives into 2D images, enabling both standard novel view synthesis and advanced inverse rendering tasks [G]. It solves the problem of efficiently rasterizing soft, view-dependent volumetric primitives while supporting complex appearance models such as physical lighting and volume data visualization [G]. What sets these methods apart from standard [3D Gaussian Splatting](1795-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) is the decoupling of geometric representation from appearance, allowing for explicit material properties (BRDF) or scalar density fields (VEG) rather than baked spherical harmonics [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
 All methods in this family share the core mechanism of projecting 3D primitives into 2D screen space and accumulating color based on depth order or spatial locality [G].
 
-1.  **Projection:** Primitives are transformed from world space to camera space [G].
-2.  **Rasterization:** Primitives are assigned to screen tiles [G]. Visibility is determined by bounding boxes or projected extents [G].
-3.  **Compositing:** Color is accumulated using front-to-back alpha blending or order-independent techniques [G].
-4.  **Sorting:** Most methods require sorting primitives by depth within tiles to ensure correct front-to-back compositing, although some variants use spatial hashing or cell-level sorting to approximate this or enable order-independent transparency [G].
+1. **Projection:** Primitives are transformed from world space to camera space [G]. The 3D covariance matrix $\Sigma$ is transformed using the view matrix $W$ and the Jacobian $J$ of the perspective projection approximation: $\Sigma_c = J W \Sigma W^T J^T$ [G]. The 2D covariance $\Sigma_{2D}$ is extracted as the upper-left $2\times2$ block [G].
+2. **Rasterization:** Primitives are assigned to screen tiles [G]. Visibility is determined by bounding boxes or projected extents [G].
+3. **Compositing:** Color is accumulated using front-to-back alpha blending or order-independent techniques [G]. The final intensity for a pixel is $I_f = \sum_{i=1}^N c_i \tilde{\sigma}_i \tilde{\mathcal{G}}_i(\mathbf{p}) \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \tilde{\sigma}_j \tilde{\mathcal{G}}_j(\mathbf{p}))$, where $c_i$ is color, $\tilde{\sigma}_i$ is opacity or density, and $\tilde{\mathcal{G}}_i(\mathbf{p})$ is the 2D Gaussian value at pixel $\mathbf{p}$ [G].
+4. **Sorting:** Most methods require sorting primitives by depth within tiles to ensure correct front-to-back compositing, although some variants use spatial hashing or cell-level sorting to approximate this or enable order-independent transparency [G].
 
